# docs/AUDIT.md 1.51 -- an atomic slot is an integer counter, and only that.
#
# The atomic family has two production points (src/runtime/runtime.c and
# src/runtime/runtime_posix.c) and, until this pin, neither of them consulted
# the slot's `type` tag before reading or writing the union:
#
#   atomic_get("y")   on `y = 1.5`     POSIX answered 4609434218613702656 (the
#                                      IEEE754 bits of 1.5); Windows answered 0.
#   atomic_add("h", 0) on `h = 2.5`    POSIX answered 4612811918334230528 and
#                                      destroyed the 2.5; Windows answered 0 and
#                                      also destroyed it, via a silent
#                                      normalise (`type = VAL_INT; ival = 0`).
#
# Both copies now answer 0 and leave a non-integer slot untouched -- the same
# answer the family already gives when the name does not resolve.  The values
# below are asserted, not just the exit code: docs/SYNTAX.md 7.2 M13 records
# that 66 of the CTest cases only look at the exit code, and this defect is
# exactly the kind that exits 0.
y = 1.5
s = "abcdef"
k = 7

g1 = atomic_get("y")
r1 = atomic_add("y", 1)
y_after = str(y)
r3 = atomic_add("y", 0)
y_zero = str(y)

g2 = atomic_get("s")
r2 = atomic_add("s", 1)
s_after = str(s)

g3 = atomic_get("k")
r4 = atomic_add("k", 5)

# docs/SYNTAX.md 7.1 D14 (2026-10, human ruling) -- a name argument that is not
# a string is a *caller error*: the call could never have worked, and answering
# 0 would be indistinguishable from success, so it raises type_mismatch.  A name
# that simply does not resolve is *runtime state* -- there is nothing there --
# and keeps answering 0.  Before D14 all four of the calls below answered 0, so
# "you passed the wrong kind of thing" and "there is no such counter" had one
# answer between them.
#
# The kind string is asserted, not the exit code.  A pin that only checked for
# "something was raised" would pass on a numeric_overflow, on an index error, or
# on any future kind; M13's numerator is exactly the count of tests that settle
# for an exit code, and this is a case that has a distinguishable answer.
try {
  n1 = atomic_get(42)
  n1 = "NO-THROW"
} catch (e1) {
  n1 = str(e1)
}
try {
  n2 = atomic_add(42, 1)
  n2 = "NO-THROW"
} catch (e2) {
  n2 = str(e2)
}
try {
  n3 = atomic_set(42, 1)
  n3 = "NO-THROW"
} catch (e3) {
  n3 = str(e3)
}
n4 = atomic_get("this_name_was_never_created")

say "atomic-slot-ok g1=" + str(g1) + " r1=" + str(r1) + " y=" + y_after + " r3=" + str(r3) + " yz=" + y_zero + " g2=" + str(g2) + " r2=" + str(r2) + " s=" + s_after + " g3=" + str(g3) + " r4=" + str(r4) + " n1=" + n1 + " n2=" + n2 + " n3=" + n3 + " n4=" + str(n4)
